# Rigid-body rotamer/hinge mechanism + dynamic hydrogen bonds (synthetic helix-loop-sheet, unfolded)

Combines two mechanisms on a synthetic, from-scratch peptide, supplied here
**fully unfolded**, meant to be pulled into shape interactively:

* `--rigidbody` (see 070.RigidBodyRotamere): springs built directly from
  `data/reducerules/ProteinAtomRigidGroups.rbody`, one pairwise spring per
  named rigid-body group (CA tetrahedron, peptide plane, chi pivots,
  hydroxyl/thiol rotations). Backbone phi/psi and every side-chain chi
  remain genuine, free rotational degrees of freedom.
* Dynamic hydrogen bonds (`hbond.enable` in `model.msp`): a distance-only
  Morse potential between donor and acceptor heavy atoms, no explicit H
  particle, no angular term (see `src/forcefield/energy/hydrogenbond.hpp`
  for the literature-derived parameters). Donor/acceptor roles come from
  `amber.hbond` (a local copy of `data/reducerules/amber.hbond`, referenced
  by `hbond.path` in `model.msp`). Each particle can be engaged in at most
  one active pair at a time, matched by mutual nearest neighbor and held
  until the pair drifts apart beyond cutoff (see
  `SpringNetwork::_assignHydrogenBondPairs`).
* Coulomb electrostatics (`coulomb.enable`) and steric exclusion
  (`steric.enable`), both already built into BioSpring, added specifically
  to help close the large starting gap that `--hbond` alone cannot (see
  below): electrostatics reaches twice as far (16 A vs hbond's 7 A) and
  acts on the construct's real AMBER partial charges (Lys/Arg positive,
  Asp/Glu negative), while steric exclusion keeps charged residues from
  collapsing into an overlapping, physically impossible clash as they are
  pulled together.

### The construct: 10-residue helix + 10-residue loop + 9-residue beta hairpin

`helix_loop_sheet.pdb` is entirely synthetic, built from scratch (no PDB
excerpt), because reproducing hydrogen-bond-driven secondary structure
formation is the actual point of this example:

* **Residues 1-10** (`AEAAKEAAKA`): ideal alpha-helix backbone (phi=-57,
  psi=-47 throughout).
* **Residues 11-20** (`GSGSGSGSNG`): flexible Gly/Ser-rich loop, no
  secondary-structure target.
* **Residues 21-29** (`VKTLTGKTI`): a beta hairpin. Note this is 9 residues,
  not 10 -- a real antiparallel strand only hydrogen-bonds every *other*
  residue (the alternating in-register/solvent-facing pattern of a pleated
  sheet), which forces any exactly-symmetric hairpin window to be
  odd-length, centered on one self-paired turn residue. There is no valid
  even-length window for a clean two-strand hairpin. This segment reuses
  the *measured* backbone dihedrals of ubiquitin's own real, independently
  validated N-terminal hairpin (residues 5-13) rather than idealized
  textbook turn values, which turned out not to keep a hairpin in register
  over its full length when tested.

Backbone coordinates were built residue by residue with the standard NeRF
(Natural Extension Reference Frame) construction from ideal bond
lengths/angles and the phi/psi values above; side chains were transplanted
as a rigid body (exact rotation+translation, so all internal bond
lengths/angles are preserved exactly) from a real ubiquitin residue of the
matching amino acid type. The folded (helix/hairpin formed) geometry was
verified numerically before use -- backbone N...O distances at the expected
i,i-4 register for the helix, and at the hairpin's cross-strand register --
then every residue's phi/psi was overridden to a single extended value
(-120, 140) to produce the actual starting structure shipped here: no
helix, no turn, no sheet register anywhere, residues that belong together
in the fold starting tens of Angstroms apart.

Two properties of this construction are worth knowing before you read
anything into a run:

* Until 2026-08-13 the shipped file carried phi=+120 psi=-140, the mirror
  image of the extended value documented above. The alpha carbons were
  correctly L all along (improper N-C-CA-CB = +122.3 deg, matching
  GKinase's +122.0), so it was a genuine L-peptide parked in the corner of
  the Ramachandran plot that L-amino acids forbid -- ff99SB penalises
  phi>0 by ~6 kJ/mol per residue. The torsions have been dialled back to
  the documented (-120, 140) by rotating about the phi/psi bonds, which
  leaves every bond length, valence angle and stereocentre untouched.
* The NeRF construction uses *ideal* valence angles, which are not AMBER's
  equilibrium angles. That no longer costs anything here -- the rigid mesh
  takes each angle's equilibrium from the structure as loaded, so an ideal
  geometry is simply the model's own minimum -- but it does mean this
  structure is not a substitute for a real one if you ever compare absolute
  valence energies against a force field.

### What to actually expect when you pull on this

This was tested end to end before shipping, at each stage, and the result
matters for how you use it interactively.

**With only `--hbond` enabled** (Morse potential, `hbond.cutoff` = 7 A) and
*no* intervention, nothing refolds on its own over a 15000-step (22.5 ps)
test run -- the backbone barely moves past its starting extended geometry.
This is not a bug in the mechanism; it is a real consequence of the model:
in the unfolded structure the helix and sheet segments start ~10-30 A
apart, far outside hbond's 7 A reach, and `viscosity` in this model only
damps motion -- nothing re-injects random thermal motion the way a real
Langevin thermostat would, so once kinetic energy is damped out (which
happens quickly) there is no more spontaneous exploration left to bring
distant segments into range.

**Adding Coulomb electrostatics (16 A reach) and steric exclusion, and
lowering friction from 0.5 to 0.05** (all in `model.msp` as shipped) does
measurably better: over an 8000-step (12 ps) test run, several backbone
N...O distances that started within electrostatic range shrank
noticeably (e.g. one hairpin pair went from 11.2 A to 9.0 A; the chain's
overall end-to-end extent dropped from ~95.5 A to ~90.1 A). But the
segments that start furthest apart (up to ~30 A for some hairpin pairs)
are still beyond electrostatics' own 16 A reach, and 12 ps of movement at
this rate is nowhere near enough to close a 10-30 A gap on its own within
any reasonable simulated time.

**In other words: you still have to bring the segments together
yourself** (pull the helix toward the sheet via the interactive MDDriver
connection) to get them within range -- electrostatics now helps carry
them the rest of the way once you have closed most of the gap, instead of
leaving hbond to do all the work alone from far away.

**Steric mode**: `steric.mode = lennard-jones-8-6Amber` -- despite the
name, this is actually AMBER's real 12-6 potential (the genuinely-8-6
"Lewitt"/"Zacharias" modes are softer, non-AMBER alternatives). This mode
originally produced single-step kinetic-energy spikes into the hundreds of
thousands of kJ.mol-1. Softening the exponent (Lewitt) only reduced the
spike by about an order of magnitude -- it turned out not to be a steric
problem at all. Reproducible logging traced a specific spike to the
hydrogen-bond mechanism: a particle "occupied" by an exclusive bond
elsewhere was excluded from all hbond force computation with everyone
else, including short-range repulsion, so it could drift arbitrarily close
to another donor/acceptor while unbonded, and only feel a (by then
enormous) corrective force once finally reassigned to that too-close
neighbor (caught in the act: THR28:TOG1 released from a distant partner
and immediately reassigned to ILE29:IN at 2.4 A, well inside the Morse
well). The fix -- an always-on, short-range-only repulsive floor between
any donor/acceptor pair, independent of engagement status
(`Particle::addHydrogenBondCoreRepulsion`), mirroring how patchy-particle
models keep an isotropic excluded-volume core entirely separate from the
exclusive directional attractive patch term -- removed the problem
outright. With it in place, 12-6 "Amber" and 8-6 "Lewitt" now produce
bit-identical trajectories over a 90 ps test here (steric's own reported
energy stays at 0.00 kJ.mol-1 throughout either way -- it is no longer
doing meaningful work, because hbond's own core repulsion now keeps
donor/acceptor pairs apart before steric would ever need to). AMBER's real
12-6 was kept since it is the mode that actually matches amber.ff/
amber.grp's own parameterization.

Once the segments are in hydrogen-bonding range, this mechanism has already been validated
(separately, on ubiquitin's own real, folded alpha helix and beta hairpin)
to correctly identify and hold the native backbone hydrogen-bond pattern --
10/10 helix i,i-4 pairs and 7/7 hairpin cross-strand pairs recovered
exactly, starting from the native fold. The open, unresolved question this
example lets you explore interactively is whether pulling this *unfolded*
chain into rough proximity is enough for --hbond to then find and lock in
the correct register on its own, or whether it settles for some other,
non-native contact instead.

### Coloring and secondary-structure view (model.vmd)

Two complementary representations on the same structure:

* **NewCartoon, colored by Structure**: VMD's built-in (STRIDE) secondary
  structure assignment, recalculated every frame during the interactive
  session (VMD does not do this automatically past the first frame, so
  `model.vmd` sets up a trace on frame updates to force it) -- this is the
  actual live view of whether the chain regains a helix ribbon and a sheet
  ribbon as you pull it. Because STRIDE only recognizes the standard
  backbone atom names, `model.vmd` renames backbone N/CA/C/O back from
  amber.grp's per-residue-type renamed forms (e.g. "EN" -> "N" for
  glutamate) right after loading, once, before the interactive session
  starts.
* **Ball-and-stick (CPK), colored by hydrogen-bond role**: grey (not a
  donor or acceptor), blue (donor only), red (acceptor only), purple (both
  -- Ser/Thr/Tyr hydroxyls), plus a thin orange `Bonds` overlay for the
  rigid-body spring network itself.

This example needs a local BioSpring build with `--rigidbody`, the hbond
mechanism (`hbond.*` settings) and the data files listed above -- see the
Makefile.

### Build and visualize

    make prep
    make vis   (VMD: NewCartoon by secondary structure + CPK by donor/acceptor role + spring-network overlay)

### Launch the simulation

    make run / make run_now
